window.docContents[245].resolve({"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Formalization-in-Lean--Lean___-Mathlib___-and-the-trusted-core":{"contents":"Lean is both a programming language and an interactive theorem prover. Its\nlogic identifies propositions with types and proofs with terms: a statement\nsuch as\n\nis a type, and a proof is a term of that type. A tactic helps construct the\nterm; the kernel checks it against the statement. This separation lets us\nuse elaborate search procedures without making their every decision part\nof the mathematical justification. A mistaken candidate still needs to pass\nthe checker.\n\nMathlib supplies the shared objects: real and complex numbers, measures and\nBochner integrals, normed spaces and continuous linear maps, derivatives and\ndistributions. Its Fourier analysis, weak topologies, spectral results, and\ncompact operators support the later arguments. Reusing these definitions\nmatters when two proofs meet. The derivative returned by the CAS and the\nderivative used by an energy theorem should describe the same operation;\notherwise every application would need another equivalence proof. The\nexpression language used for computation therefore comes with theorems\nconnecting its interpretation to ordinary Mathlib functions.\n\nCAS, bare cega, ordinary proofs, and the numerical checks are checked in the\nkernel. Users can explicitly opt into cega [fast] for closed finite certificates\nand polynomial interval evaluation; under the fast build profile these checks\nadditionally trust Lean's compiler and native evaluation, and the results that use\nthem inherit the generated native axioms. The default build profile checks them in\nthe kernel. If these routes cannot\ncertify the goal, the tactic falls back to kernel CEGA; the exponential,\nlogarithmic, and other proof-bearing planner routes remain unchanged.\n\nThe kernel checks the implication we stated, relative to the foundations and\nassumptions it uses. Choosing that statement is still our responsibility.\nA proof of a residual identity cannot detect that the author meant to ask\nfor the initial-boundary value problem. Nor can it decide whether an equation\nmodels a particular fluid or whether the prose describes a cited paper\ncorrectly.\n\nWe follow  from classical formulas through\nSobolev spaces to weak equations, variational methods, conservation laws,\nand Hamilton–Jacobi theory.\nPDE problem records distinguish\nthe residual, initial data, boundary data, and regularity of a solution.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction\u0009Formalization in Lean","header":"1.4.1. Lean, Mathlib, and the trusted core","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Formalization-in-Lean--Lean___-Mathlib___-and-the-trusted-core"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--The-Dirichlet-example-with-one-fewer-derivative":{"contents":"Return to  on  with zero endpoint values. Multiplying a\nclassical solution by  and integrating by parts\ngives\n\n\n\nThis equation only needs  and .\nDensity and Cauchy–Schwarz extend the test identity to every\n. The boundary condition is carried by the space\n, defined by closure of compactly supported smooth functions.\nIt would not follow from the interior test identity alone.\n\nThere is also an energy interpretation. For\n\n\n\nthe weak equation says that the first variation vanishes. Expanding the\nsquare then gives  for every\n, so a weak solution minimizes this energy.\nFor , the same arch  from Foundations satisfies both\nformulations. For rougher data, the weak formulation still makes sense\nbefore a classical second derivative has been established. A regularity\ntheorem is what permits the later return to a pointwise equation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009The analytic language of weak solutions","header":"4.1.1. The Dirichlet example with one fewer derivative","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--The-analytic-language-of-weak-solutions--The-Dirichlet-example-with-one-fewer-derivative"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#symbolic-sine-example":{"contents":"The profile  solves two different equations. We use it as\na small example of the wave and\ntransport interfaces:\n\n\n\nThe checked examples live in PDE/Examples/Hyperbolic. The wave theorem\nrecords the zero residual on all spacetime; the transport theorem also\nchecks the assigned value  by substitution. That part of\nits proof is a slice equality, with no limiting argument. The derivative\ncalculation is short:\n and  cancel, while\n.\n\nFor the wave calculation, the\nderivative bridge transfers the symbolic\nidentity to ordinary derivatives. The transport example applies the checked\nderivative tactic directly to the ordinary function. Both calculations then\nfeed the library's PDE predicates.\n\nWe can also integrate the residual. For this profile, the symbolic residual\nequals the ordinary advective derivative at every point, so their integrals\nagree on every set and both vanish.\n\nWriting  for the reflected residual and\n, the example proves\n\n\n\nfor every  and every set .\nA conservation-law weak formulation needs more, including test functions\nand the initial or boundary terms.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Exact Symbolic Calculus\u0009Symbolic PDE Theorem API","header":"10.6.4.1. One profile, two equations","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Exact-Symbolic-Calculus/#symbolic-sine-example"}});